`bytes.tokenizerId`.

The assertion (from `packages/core/test/cache.test.ts`, seq of the recorded events):

```ts
const bytes = new ProviderCacheSimulator({
  tokenizer: new ExactTokenizer('bytes-v0', (text) => text.length),
}).submit(prompt);
...
expect(bytes.tokenizerId).toBe('bytes-v0');
```

One precision on the premise, though: the `bytes` object doesn't actually appear in the byte-identical/re-assemble test itself ("is byte-identical across a config-identical re-assemble and is read from cache on the second submission…", line 185). It belongs to the test immediately *after* it in the same `Zone A — the frozen prefix (§10 rule 5, D5)` describe block — "records the injected tokenizer id on every outcome so swapping tokenizers is a deliberate diff rather than a silent shift of every recorded range (§17)" (line 209). That test builds two simulator outcomes over the same prompt — `heuristic` (the harness's default tokenizer) and `bytes` (an exact byte-length tokenizer registered under the id `'bytes-v0'`) — and asserts `bytes.tokenizerId === 'bytes-v0'`, i.e. the simulator stamps the tokenizer's registered id onto every outcome, so a tokenizer swap shows up as an explicit diff in recorded cache ranges rather than silently shifting them.

The property is **`tokenizerId`**.

In `packages/core/test/cache.test.ts` (line 220) the assertion reads:

```ts
expect(bytes.tokenizerId).toBe('bytes-v0');
```

where `bytes` is a `ProviderCacheSimulator` constructed with an injected exact tokenizer:

```ts
const bytes = new ProviderCacheSimulator({
  tokenizer: new ExactTokenizer('bytes-v0', (text) => text.length),
}).submit(prompt);
```

One precision note: the `'bytes-v0'` assertion actually lives in the *neighboring* test — the one at line 209, "records the injected tokenizer id on every outcome so swapping tokenizers is a deliberate diff rather than a silent shift of every recorded range (§17)". The test you described ("is byte-identical across a config-identical re-assemble and is read from cache on the second submission…", line 185) submits the prompt twice and asserts prefix stability/cache reads, but doesn't touch the `bytes` object. The two tests sit back-to-back in the `'Zone A — the frozen prefix (§10 rule 5, D5)'` describe block, which is likely why they blur together.

The purpose of the `tokenizerId` assertion: the simulator stamps whichever tokenizer was injected onto every outcome, so that if the tokenizer is ever swapped, the recorded token ranges change as an explicit, deliberate diff (`bytes.total` differing from `heuristic.total`, while the segment block-id layout stays equal) rather than silently shifting.

